# Binary Framing Specification (`Serializer` & `SerialIter`)

The XRPL binary wire format uses standard framing primitives for **Field IDs** and **Variable-Length (VL) Prefixes**.

## 0. JSON Schema & Wire Framing Mapping Table

| JSON Property | C++ Symbol / Function | Wire Encoding | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `header` | `Serializer::addFieldID(type, name)` | 1 to 3 bytes | `(type < 16 && name < 16)` $\to$ `(type << 4) \| name` |
| `vl` | `Serializer::addVL` / `encodeLengthLength` | 1 to 3 bytes | Length prefix: 1 byte ($0..192$), 2 bytes ($193..12480$), 3 bytes ($12481..918744$) |
| `value` | `Serializer::add*` / `field->add(s)` | $N$ payload bytes | Raw field value payload in hexadecimal |
| `name` | `SField::getName()` | N/A (metadata) | Canonical SField identifier |

---

## 1. Field ID Header Framing

Every serialized field begins with a Field ID header encoding its `(fieldType, fieldValue)` pair:

* **1-Byte Header**: When both `type < 16` and `field < 16`:
  $$\text{header} = (\text{type} \ll 4) \mid \text{field}$$
* **2-Byte Header (Type $\ge 16$)**: When `type >= 16` and `field < 16`:
  $$\text{byte}_0 = \text{field}, \quad \text{byte}_1 = \text{type}$$
* **2-Byte Header (Field $\ge 16$)**: When `type < 16` and `field >= 16`:
  $$\text{byte}_0 = \text{type} \ll 4, \quad \text{byte}_1 = \text{field}$$
* **3-Byte Header**: When both `type >= 16` and `field >= 16`:
  $$\text{byte}_0 = 0x00, \quad \text{byte}_1 = \text{type}, \quad \text{byte}_2 = \text{field}$$

